      "In 1610 Galileo Galilei published The Starry Messenger, reporting what his telescope showed: mountains on the Moon, many more stars than the eye can see, and four moons circling Jupiter. He soon observed that Venus shows a full set of phases, which the old Earth-centred system of Ptolemy could not explain. Galileo became a public champion of the Sun-centred system Nicolaus Copernicus had published in 1543.",
      "Galileo's observations did not settle the question by themselves. The phases of Venus also fit the system of Tycho Brahe, in which the planets circle the Sun while the Sun circles a stationary Earth. If the Earth moved, astronomers expected the fixed stars to shift slightly over the year, but no such stellar parallax was measured until 1838. Galileo's own favourite proof, that the Earth's motion causes the tides, was wrong.",
      "In 1615 Galileo wrote his Letter to the Grand Duchess Christina, arguing that Scripture speaks in the language of ordinary people and that the Bible teaches how one goes to heaven, not how the heavens go. In the same year Cardinal Robert Bellarmine wrote that if there were a true demonstration of the Earth's motion, the Church would have to reinterpret the passages that seemed to contradict it, but that until such a proof was shown the traditional reading should stand. In 1616 Copernicus's book was suspended until corrected, and Galileo was told not to hold or defend the doctrine.",
      "In 1632 Galileo published his Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems. Its defender of the old view, Simplicio, was given an argument that Pope Urban VIII, once Galileo's admirer, had urged him to include, and the Pope was reportedly offended. In 1633 the Inquisition found Galileo vehemently suspected of heresy. He was made to abjure and spent the rest of his life under house arrest near Florence, dying in 1642. The story that he muttered \"and yet it moves\" is probably a later legend.",
      "In the nineteenth century writers such as John William Draper and Andrew Dickson White made the affair the founding example of a permanent war between science and religion. Many historians now reject this conflict thesis. They point out that the Church had long supported astronomy, that the dispute ran through questions of evidence, of who had authority to interpret Scripture during the Counter-Reformation, and of personal and court politics. Others reply that whatever the details, an institution used force to silence a scientist for a true claim. In 1992 Pope John Paul II acknowledged that Galileo's judges had erred."
