      "Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859. He argued that species change over long periods by natural selection: individuals vary, more are born than can survive, and those whose variations suit their conditions leave more offspring. Darwin had worked on the idea for about twenty years before publishing; he was prompted to act when Alfred Russel Wallace sent him an essay containing a similar theory, and their papers were presented together in 1858.",
      "The book provoked a varied response. Some clergy, such as the novelist Charles Kingsley, welcomed it as compatible with a creator who made a world able to make itself. The American botanist Asa Gray argued that selection could be the means of a design. Some scientists, such as the anatomist Richard Owen, objected on scientific grounds. Others saw it as an attack on the special dignity of human beings, a question Darwin addressed directly in The Descent of Man in 1871.",
      "At a meeting in Oxford in 1860, Bishop Samuel Wilberforce and Thomas Henry Huxley clashed over the theory. Wilberforce reportedly asked whether Huxley claimed descent from an ape through his grandfather or his grandmother, and Huxley reportedly answered that he would rather have an ape for an ancestor than a man who used his gifts to obscure the truth. No verbatim record exists, the accounts were written down later, and historians think the exchange grew in the retelling into a symbol of science defeating religion.",
      "Huxley and his allies also used the debate to establish science as a profession independent of clergy, who had long dominated English learning. On this reading the controversy was partly about who should hold intellectual authority in Victorian society, not only about the truth of a theory.",
