    "instructions": "The examiner will ask where you draw lines on changing human genes and why. Defend your positions against hard cases, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Scientists have been able to alter DNA since the 1970s. In 1975 researchers met at Asilomar in California and agreed on voluntary safety guidelines for recombinant DNA work, an early example of scientists regulating their own field. In 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier described how the CRISPR-Cas9 system could be used to cut DNA at chosen places, making gene editing far cheaper and more precise; they received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2020.",
      "Ethicists distinguish somatic editing, which changes cells in one patient's body and is not inherited, from germline editing, which changes embryos, eggs or sperm so that the change passes to future generations. Somatic therapies for inherited blood disorders have been approved in several countries. Germline editing is prohibited or restricted in many countries, partly because the people most affected cannot consent and errors would be inherited.",
      "In 2018 the Chinese researcher He Jiankui announced the birth of twin girls whose embryos he had edited to disable the CCR5 gene, aiming to make them resistant to HIV. The work was widely condemned as unsafe and unnecessary, since other ways to prevent transmission existed, and he was sentenced to prison in China in 2019.",
      "A second distinction is between therapy, which treats or prevents disease, and enhancement, which aims to improve traits beyond normal health, such as memory or height. Critics say the line is blurry: is preventing a high risk of heart disease therapy or enhancement? Some, like the philosopher Julian Savulescu, argue that parents have reason to choose the children with the best prospects. Others, like Michael Sandel, argue that a drive to design our children erodes an attitude of accepting life as a gift and would change parenthood itself.",
      "Further worries concern justice and history. If enhancements were expensive, they might turn economic inequality into biological inequality. The twentieth-century eugenics movements, which led to forced sterilizations in several countries, make many people wary of any project to improve the human population. Some disability-rights advocates argue that editing out conditions sends a message about the worth of people who live with them, while others reply that preventing suffering is not a judgment on anyone's value.",
