    "instructions": "The examiner will ask who owes what in responding to climate change and why. Defend your principles and test them on hard cases, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Climate change raises questions of fairness as well as science. The emissions that warm the planet come unevenly from different countries and people, while the harms, such as heat, drought, flooding and sea-level rise, often fall hardest on poorer countries and communities that emitted little.",
      "The United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change of 1992 states the principle of common but differentiated responsibilities and respective capabilities: every country shares the task, but not equally. Countries that industrialized early account for a large share of cumulative emissions since the nineteenth century, while today China emits the most each year and per-person emissions still vary greatly between countries. Under the Paris Agreement of 2015 every country sets its own nationally determined contribution, with the aim of keeping warming well below 2 degrees Celsius and pursuing 1.5 degrees. At the 2022 conference in Egypt, countries agreed to set up a fund for loss and damage in vulnerable countries.",
      "Philosophers have proposed several principles for sharing the burden. Polluter pays says those who caused the problem should pay to fix it. Critics reply that many past emitters are dead and that before the science was widely known they could not have known the harm. Beneficiary pays answers that people today still benefit from wealth built on past emissions. Ability to pay, defended by the philosopher Henry Shue among others, says those with the most resources should bear the most, and that no one should be asked to give up emissions needed for a decent minimum of life.",
      "Climate policy also asks how much weight future people deserve. Economists compare costs now with damages later by applying a discount rate. The Stern Review of 2006 used a very low rate and called for strong early action, while economists such as William Nordhaus argued for higher rates and a more gradual path. The choice of rate is partly an ethical judgment about whether the welfare of people in the future counts less simply because it is later.",
      "Finally, there is the question of individuals. The philosopher Walter Sinnott-Armstrong argued that a single person's joyride makes no detectable difference to climate harm, so the duty lies with governments rather than individuals. Others reply that individual choices add up, shape norms and express integrity, and that citizens have duties to push for collective action."
    ],
