    "passage": [
      "Mathematicians often speak as if they discover things: a theorem was true before anyone proved it, and a proof reveals it. Yet mathematics is also built from definitions and axioms that people choose. The philosophy of mathematics asks which description is right, or whether both capture something.",
      "Platonism holds that numbers, sets and other mathematical objects exist independently of human minds, outside space and time, and that mathematicians discover truths about them. The view takes its name from Plato's theory of Forms. Kurt Gödel and G. H. Hardy expressed Platonist views. Platonism explains why mathematical truths seem necessary, objective and the same for everyone. The philosopher Paul Benacerraf posed a famous difficulty in 1973: if mathematical objects are abstract and causally inert, how could we ever come to know anything about them?",
      "Other views treat mathematics as a human creation. Formalism, associated with David Hilbert's programme, treats mathematics as the manipulation of symbols according to rules, without asking what the symbols refer to. Intuitionism, developed by L. E. J. Brouwer, holds that mathematical objects are mental constructions, and it rejects some classical proofs that assert existence without constructing anything. Fictionalists such as Hartry Field argue that talk of numbers is a useful fiction. The nineteenth-century mathematician Leopold Kronecker is reported to have said that God made the integers and all else is the work of man.",
      "The history of geometry is often cited in this debate. For two thousand years Euclid's parallel postulate seemed self-evident. In the nineteenth century mathematicians including Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Riemann built consistent geometries in which it fails. Some took this to show that axioms are free choices rather than discoveries. Yet Riemann's geometry later proved to be the mathematics Einstein needed for general relativity.",
      "In 1960 the physicist Eugene Wigner wrote of the unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics in the natural sciences: structures developed for their own sake, such as complex numbers, turn out to describe the physical world with uncanny accuracy. Platonists see this as evidence that mathematics captures real structure. Others reply that we keep the mathematics that works and forget the rest, or that our mathematics grew out of experience of the world in the first place. W. V. O. Quine and Hilary Putnam argued that because mathematics is indispensable to our best science, we are committed to the existence of mathematical objects."
    ],
