        "facts": ["Platonism explains why mathematical truths seem necessary and objective.", "Gödel and Hardy expressed Platonist views.", "Benacerraf asked how we could know about causally inert abstract objects.", "A strong answer responds to the problem of knowledge, not only to the feeling of discovery."],
        "misconceptions": ["Platonism claims numbers are physical things somewhere in the universe.", "Because a proof feels like discovery, Platonism must be true."] },
      { "key": "invention-views", "title": "Evaluate views that mathematics is made", "description": "Compares formalism, intuitionism or fictionalism and judges what they explain and miss.",
        "concept": "invention-views", "definition": "Views that treat mathematics as a human creation: rule-governed symbol games, mental constructions or useful fictions.",
        "facts": ["Formalism treats mathematics as manipulating symbols by rules.", "Brouwer's intuitionism treats mathematical objects as mental constructions and rejects some non-constructive proofs.", "Fictionalism treats talk of numbers as a useful fiction.", "A strong answer explains why invented mathematics is not therefore arbitrary."],
