        "facts": ["Euclid's parallel postulate long seemed self-evident.", "Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Riemann built consistent geometries without it.", "Riemann's geometry later served general relativity.", "A strong answer says what the episode supports for each side."],
        "misconceptions": ["Non-Euclidean geometry proved Euclid wrong.", "If several geometries are consistent, none can describe reality."] },
      { "key": "effectiveness", "title": "Explain the effectiveness of mathematics", "description": "Defends an explanation of why abstract mathematics describes the physical world so well.",
        "concept": "unreasonable-effectiveness", "definition": "Wigner's puzzle that mathematics developed for its own sake so often describes nature with great accuracy.",
        "facts": ["Wigner raised the puzzle in 1960.", "Complex numbers, developed abstractly, are central to modern physics.", "Sceptics note selection: we keep the mathematics that works and forget the rest.", "The Quine-Putnam indispensability argument infers mathematical objects from their role in science."],
