    "instructions": "The examiner will ask whether science and the humanities are really divided and what each owes the other. Defend your view, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "In 1959 the British novelist and former physicist C. P. Snow gave the Rede Lecture at Cambridge, published as The Two Cultures and the Scientific Revolution. He argued that the intellectual life of the West had split into two groups, literary intellectuals and scientists, who barely understood or respected each other. He said he had asked educated people whether they could describe the Second Law of Thermodynamics, and that this was the scientific equivalent of asking whether they had read a work of Shakespeare.",
      "Snow's argument was not only about manners. He claimed that a literary establishment indifferent to science was ill-equipped to govern a modern society, and that the scientific and industrial revolution was the main hope for relieving poverty in the poorer nations. He suggested that the humanities had at times been suspicious of progress itself.",
      "In 1962 the literary critic F. R. Leavis replied in a lecture of unusual harshness, attacking Snow's standing as a novelist and thinker. Behind the personal attack was a real disagreement: Leavis held that literature and criticism keep alive the questions of value and purpose that a society must ask about its own progress, and that a culture cannot be judged by its material standard of living alone.",
      "The quarrel had a Victorian precedent. In 1880 Thomas Henry Huxley argued that science deserved a central place in education, and in 1882 Matthew Arnold replied that the study of letters remained essential because it relates knowledge to our sense of conduct and beauty. In 1963 Snow himself suggested that a third culture, including the social sciences, might bridge the gap.",
      "Later episodes renewed the debate. In 1996 the physicist Alan Sokal had a deliberately nonsensical article accepted by the cultural studies journal Social Text, and presented it as evidence of careless thinking about science in parts of the humanities. In 1998 the biologist E. O. Wilson called in Consilience for uniting all knowledge, including ethics and the arts, under explanations continuous with natural science. Critics responded that interpretation, meaning and value cannot be reduced to scientific explanation without losing what makes them what they are."
    ],
