    "title": "What Is Art?",
    "category": "arts-languages",
    "subject": "Aesthetics",
    "topic": "Defining art",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask what makes something a work of art. Test each definition against examples, defend your own view, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "People have long tried to say what all works of art have in common. The oldest answer, found in Plato and Aristotle, is that art is mimesis, imitation or representation. Plato distrusted it as a copy of a copy, far from the truth; Aristotle thought imitation natural to human beings and a source of learning and pleasure. But much music, architecture and abstract painting represents nothing.",
      "Expression theories hold that art communicates or clarifies feeling. In What Is Art? (1897) Leo Tolstoy argued that art is the activity in which a person, having experienced a feeling, transmits it to others so that they are infected by it, and that art should unite people. He condemned much celebrated art of his day as obscure and exclusive. R. G. Collingwood later argued that art is the artist's working out of an emotion that was at first unclear.",
      "Formalism looks to the work itself. In 1914 the critic Clive Bell proposed that what all visual art shares is significant form, arrangements of lines and colours that stir a distinctive aesthetic emotion, regardless of subject matter.",
      "In 1917 Marcel Duchamp submitted a mass-produced porcelain urinal, signed \"R. Mutt\" and titled Fountain, to an exhibition in New York that had promised to show any work whose fee was paid. It was not displayed. Duchamp's readymades seemed to show that an object could become art without being made, beautiful or expressive by the artist, and later critics treated Fountain as one of the most influential works of the century.",
      "Such cases inspired institutional theories. Arthur Danto, struck by Andy Warhol's Brillo Box (1964), which looked almost exactly like ordinary supermarket boxes, argued that what makes something art is a theory and an atmosphere of art history, which he called the artworld. George Dickie argued that a work of art is an artifact presented to an artworld public. Critics object that this seems circular and makes art depend on insiders' decisions.",
      "Others doubt that art can be defined at all. In 1956 Morris Weitz, drawing on Wittgenstein's idea of family resemblance, argued that art is an open concept whose members share overlapping similarities but no single essence, and that each new movement stretches it further. Recent images made by machine-learning systems add a new test case for every theory."
    ],
