    "title": "Impressionism as Rebellion",
    "category": "arts-languages",
    "subject": "Art History",
    "topic": "Impressionism",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask how rebellious the Impressionists really were and what their paintings meant. Defend your interpretation with examples, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "In nineteenth-century France, artistic careers depended on the Salon, the official exhibition associated with the Académie des Beaux-Arts. Its juries favoured smooth finish and a hierarchy of genres in which large paintings of history, myth and religion ranked above landscapes and scenes of everyday life. Rejection could cost an artist buyers and reputation.",
      "In 1863 so many works were rejected that Emperor Napoleon III allowed a Salon des Refusés to show them. Édouard Manet's Le Déjeuner sur l'herbe, showing a nude woman picnicking with clothed modern men, caused a scandal there. Manet became a hero to younger painters, though he never showed in their independent exhibitions.",
      "In 1874 a group including Claude Monet, Pierre-Auguste Renoir, Camille Pissarro, Edgar Degas and Berthe Morisot organized their own exhibition, in the former studio of the photographer Nadar. A critic, Louis Leroy, mocked Monet's Impression, Sunrise and called the group Impressionists, and they adopted the name. Eight group exhibitions followed until 1886.",
      "The Impressionists often painted outdoors, used visible, broken brushstrokes and bright colour to record changing light, and took subjects from modern life: railway stations, boulevards, cafés, suburban leisure and middle-class interiors. Morisot and Mary Cassatt painted women's domestic and social worlds from the inside. Critics at first called the work unfinished.",
      "Historians disagree about how rebellious this was. Several Impressionists still sent work to the Salon when it suited them; Renoir and Monet both did around 1880. They depended on dealers such as Paul Durand-Ruel and on private collectors, so independence from the state also meant entry into a commercial market. Some see their bright scenes of leisure as a celebration of bourgeois modern Paris, newly rebuilt under Baron Haussmann; others, following the art historian T. J. Clark, read them as registering the anonymity, spectacle and class divisions of that city. Pissarro held anarchist sympathies, while Degas held strongly conservative views, so the group shared no single politics."
    ],
