        "facts": ["The Salon and Académie controlled artistic reputations.", "The group held eight independent exhibitions from 1874 to 1886.", "Several members still submitted to the Salon, including Renoir and Monet around 1880.", "A strong answer weighs both the risk they took and the compromises they made."],
        "misconceptions": ["The Impressionists completely rejected the Salon from the start.", "Wanting to sell paintings means they were not really rebels."] },
      { "key": "technique-meaning", "title": "Interpret technique as a statement", "description": "Argues whether broken brushwork and painting outdoors were themselves a challenge to accepted values.",
        "concept": "technique-meaning", "definition": "The idea that a way of painting can carry meaning, such as valuing perception and the passing moment over finish and permanence.",
        "facts": ["Impressionists used visible, broken brushstrokes and bright colour.", "They often painted outdoors to record changing light.", "Critics at first called the work unfinished.", "A strong answer connects technique to a claim about what painting is for."],
        "misconceptions": ["Impressionists painted loosely because they lacked skill.", "Technique is only a matter of method and carries no meaning."] },
      { "key": "modern-life", "title": "Decide whether they celebrated or critiqued modern life", "description": "Interprets the scenes of leisure and city life as celebration, critique or neither.",
        "concept": "modern-life", "definition": "Competing readings of Impressionist scenes of Paris and its suburbs as celebrations of bourgeois modernity or records of its tensions.",
        "facts": ["Subjects included railway stations, boulevards, cafés and suburban leisure.", "Paris had recently been rebuilt under Haussmann.", "T. J. Clark read the paintings as registering class and spectacle in the new city.", "Morisot and Cassatt painted women's social worlds from the inside."],
        "misconceptions": ["Pretty pictures cannot have anything to say about society.", "Every Impressionist shared the same political views."] },
      { "key": "market-independence", "title": "Assess independence and the art market", "description": "Evaluates whether leaving the state system for dealers and collectors made artists freer.",
