  {
    "title": "Picasso's Guernica and Art as Protest",
    "category": "arts-languages",
    "subject": "Art History",
    "topic": "Political art",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask what Guernica means and whether art can work as protest. Defend your reading with details of the painting, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "On 26 April 1937, during the Spanish Civil War, aircraft of the German Condor Legion, with Italian support, bombed the Basque town of Guernica on behalf of Francisco Franco's Nationalist forces. The attack killed many civilians and destroyed much of the town. News reports and photographs spread quickly around the world.",
      "Pablo Picasso had already been commissioned by the government of the Spanish Republic to make a mural for its pavilion at the 1937 International Exposition in Paris. After the bombing he painted Guernica in a matter of weeks. The canvas is very large, about three and a half metres high and nearly eight metres wide, and painted only in black, white and greys, which some viewers compare to newsprint.",
      "The painting shows no aircraft and no town in any realistic way. Instead it presents a crowded, broken space: a bull, a screaming wounded horse, a mother holding a dead child, a fallen figure with a broken sword, a woman leaning in through a window with a lamp, and a light overhead that some read as an eye or an electric bulb. Picasso resisted fixed interpretations. Asked about the bull and the horse, he suggested they were simply a bull and a horse and that viewers should make of them what they would.",
      "After the exposition the painting toured to raise support for the Republic and then stayed for decades at the Museum of Modern Art in New York. Picasso asked that it not go to Spain until democracy was restored; it arrived in 1981, after Franco's death, and it now hangs in the Museo Reina Sofía in Madrid. A tapestry copy hung at the United Nations in New York, and in 2003 it was reportedly covered during press statements on the coming war in Iraq, which many took as proof of the image's continuing power.",
      "Interpreters disagree about how the painting works. Some argue that its distortion and refusal of specific detail make it a universal image of the suffering of war, which is why it has been taken up by protesters ever since. Others argue that this universality drains away the specific event and its perpetrators. Some note that a work commissioned by a government at war raises the question of where protest ends and propaganda begins. And some doubt that any painting can change what armies do, while others reply that art shapes memory and how events are judged."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "symbols", "title": "Interpret the painting's symbols", "description": "Offers and defends readings of figures such as the bull, the horse and the light, and judges the value of their ambiguity.",
        "concept": "ambiguous-symbolism", "definition": "Images whose meaning is left open by the artist, inviting several readings rather than one.",
        "facts": ["The painting includes a bull, a wounded horse, a mother with a dead child, a fallen figure with a broken sword and a woman with a lamp.", "Picasso resisted fixing the meaning of the bull and horse.", "The monochrome palette is often compared to newsprint.", "A strong answer grounds a reading in visible details and admits alternatives."],
        "misconceptions": ["Every figure has one correct meaning the viewer must decode.", "If the artist refused to explain, any reading is as good as any other."] },
      { "key": "universal-specific", "title": "Judge universality against specificity", "description": "Argues whether the painting's abstraction strengthens its protest or blurs the real event.",
