      "We disagree about beauty constantly, yet we also argue about it as if someone could be right. If beauty were merely a matter of personal liking, arguing about it would make no more sense than arguing about whether one likes coffee. If it were an objective property, it is hard to say how it could be measured.",
      "Plato treated beauty as objective and ultimately as a Form. In the Symposium, Diotima describes a ladder of love that rises from the beauty of one body to all bodies, to beautiful souls, laws and knowledge, and finally to beauty itself.",
      "In his essay Of the Standard of Taste (1757), David Hume began from the view that beauty is no quality in things themselves but exists in the mind that contemplates them. Yet he argued that there is a standard of taste: the joint verdict of true judges, who have delicate sentiment, practice, experience in comparing works, good sense and freedom from prejudice. He told a story from Don Quixote of two wine tasters who detect a taste of leather and of iron in a cask, and are mocked until a key on a leather thong is found at the bottom. Critics ask how we identify true judges without already knowing which works are good.",
      "In the Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), Immanuel Kant distinguished the agreeable, which simply pleases me, from the beautiful. A judgment of beauty, he argued, is disinterested, not based on desire to own or use the object, and it claims universal agreement even though it rests on feeling rather than on concepts or proof. When I call something beautiful I speak as if everyone ought to agree.",
