    "instructions": "The examiner will ask what the myths of Prometheus and other defiers of the gods mean. Defend your interpretation with the stories, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The earliest full accounts of Prometheus come from the poet Hesiod, around 700 BCE, in the Theogony and the Works and Days. At a sacrifice at Mecone, Prometheus divides an ox and tricks Zeus into choosing the portion of bones wrapped in glistening fat, leaving the meat for humans. In anger Zeus withholds fire; Prometheus steals it and brings it to humans hidden in a fennel stalk. Zeus punishes Prometheus by chaining him to a rock, where an eagle eats his liver, which grows back, until Heracles later frees him. Zeus punishes humanity by sending Pandora, whose jar releases evils into the world.",
      "Hesiod tells the story as a warning: it is not possible to deceive or escape the mind of Zeus, and human labour and hardship follow from Prometheus's cleverness.",
      "The tragedy Prometheus Bound, traditionally attributed to Aeschylus though its authorship is disputed, gives a different picture. Prometheus is a suffering benefactor who has given humans not only fire but writing, numbers, medicine and the other arts, and Zeus is a new and harsh ruler. Prometheus refuses to submit and holds a secret that threatens Zeus's power.",
      "Greek myth contains many other stories of mortals who challenge the gods. Arachne, in the version told by the Roman poet Ovid, challenges Athena to a weaving contest and is turned into a spider. Niobe boasts that her children make her greater than the goddess Leto, and her children are killed. Icarus flies too near the sun on wings his father made. Many readers take these as warnings against hubris, overweening pride. Others note that the stories often show the gods as jealous and cruel, so the warning may be less about virtue than about power.",
      "Later writers reshaped Prometheus as a hero of human freedom and creativity. Goethe's poem Prometheus has him defy Zeus and shape humans in his own image. Percy Bysshe Shelley wrote the lyrical drama Prometheus Unbound (1820), in which Prometheus resists tyranny. Mary Shelley subtitled Frankenstein (1818) The Modern Prometheus, linking the myth to the dangers of scientific ambition. Albert Camus later made Sisyphus, endlessly pushing his rock, a figure of defiant human dignity. Today Prometheus is often invoked in arguments about technology."
    ],
