    "title": "Architecture and Power",
    "category": "arts-languages",
    "subject": "Architecture",
    "topic": "Architecture and politics",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask how buildings and cities express and exercise power. Defend your interpretations with examples, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Rulers have always built to impress. The pyramids of Egypt, the forums and triumphal arches of Rome and the cathedrals of medieval Europe displayed the power of rulers, empires and churches in stone. In 1682 Louis XIV moved his court to Versailles, where the palace, its Hall of Mirrors and its vast gardens staged the king's authority and kept the nobility close and dependent on royal favour.",
      "Between 1853 and 1870, under Napoleon III, Baron Georges-Eugène Haussmann rebuilt much of Paris, cutting wide straight boulevards through crowded medieval districts. It is often said that the boulevards were designed to let troops move quickly and make revolutionary barricades harder to build. Historians debate how far this motive mattered compared with sanitation, traffic, property speculation and imperial prestige, and note that the rebuilding pushed many poorer residents out of the centre.",
      "Totalitarian regimes made architecture central to their image. Albert Speer designed monumental buildings for Nazi Germany and later described a theory of ruin value, building so that even ruins would impress future ages. Yet the same neoclassical columns appear in the public buildings of democracies, such as those in Washington, D.C., which raises the question of whether a style carries any politics of its own or only the meanings people give it.",
      "Architecture can also shape behaviour. Jeremy Bentham designed the panopticon, a prison in which a central watchtower lets a guard see every cell while prisoners cannot tell whether they are being watched. In Discipline and Punish (1975) Michel Foucault made it a model of how modern institutions such as schools, hospitals and factories discipline people through visibility. Winston Churchill, arguing in 1943 for rebuilding the bombed House of Commons in its old cramped, adversarial form, said that we shape our buildings and afterwards our buildings shape us.",
      "Planned capitals show both hopes and limits. Brasília, designed by Lúcio Costa and Oscar Niemeyer and inaugurated in 1960, was meant to embody a modern, egalitarian Brazil; critics say its scale and reliance on cars separated government from ordinary life. Today many societies argue over public monuments to figures linked with slavery, colonialism or dictatorship, debating whether to keep, explain, move or remove them."
    ],
    "objectives": [
