        "facts": ["Speer designed monumental neoclassical buildings for Nazi Germany.", "Neoclassical columns also appear in democratic capitals such as Washington, D.C.", "Speer described a theory of ruin value.", "A strong answer distinguishes a style's form from its uses and associations."],
        "misconceptions": ["Neoclassical architecture is inherently fascist.", "Buildings are neutral and never carry political meaning."] },
      { "key": "haussmann", "title": "Interpret the motives behind Haussmann's Paris", "description": "Weighs military, sanitary, economic and symbolic explanations of the rebuilding of Paris.",
        "concept": "haussmannization", "definition": "The rebuilding of Paris under Napoleon III and Haussmann from 1853 to 1870 with wide boulevards and new infrastructure.",
        "facts": ["Wide boulevards replaced crowded medieval districts.", "A common claim is that they eased troop movement against barricades.", "Historians also stress sanitation, traffic, speculation and prestige.", "Many poorer residents were pushed out of the centre."],
        "misconceptions": ["Haussmann rebuilt Paris only to fight revolutionaries.", "Because the city became healthier, the rebuilding had no political purpose."] },
      { "key": "shaping-behaviour", "title": "Decide whether buildings shape us", "description": "Evaluates the panopticon and Churchill's claim that buildings shape the people in them.",
        "concept": "architecture-and-behaviour", "definition": "The claim that the design of spaces influences how people act, think and relate to power.",
        "facts": ["Bentham's panopticon lets a guard see prisoners who cannot tell if they are watched.", "Foucault used it as a model of modern disciplinary institutions.", "Churchill defended the Commons' adversarial layout in 1943.", "A strong answer considers how much people resist or reinterpret spaces."],
        "misconceptions": ["Architecture determines behaviour completely.", "The design of a room makes no difference to how people act in it."] },
      { "key": "monuments", "title": "Judge what to do with contested monuments", "description": "Defends a principle for monuments to figures linked with slavery, colonialism or dictatorship.",
        "concept": "contested-monuments", "definition": "Public memorials whose subjects are now judged by many to represent injustice.",
        "facts": ["Many societies now debate keeping, explaining, moving or removing such monuments.", "A monument honours its subject as well as recording history.", "Removing a monument does not erase written history.", "A strong answer applies its principle consistently to a hard case."],
