  {
    "title": "Music and Meaning",
    "category": "arts-languages",
    "subject": "Aesthetics",
    "topic": "Philosophy of music",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask what, if anything, music means and how it moves us. No music theory is needed; defend your view with pieces you know, and say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Music without words seems to say something, yet it is hard to say what. A melody can sound sad, triumphant or anxious, but it does not describe anything or state any fact. Philosophers have long asked how music can have meaning and why it affects us so strongly.",
      "In the Republic, Plato has Socrates argue that musical modes shape character, and that the ideal city should keep only modes that encourage courage and self-control while banning those thought to make people soft or sorrowful. On this view music is powerful enough to be a matter of public concern.",
      "In On the Musically Beautiful (1854) the critic Eduard Hanslick argued that the content of music is tonally moving forms. Music cannot represent definite emotions such as love or anger, which require thoughts about objects; at most it can present the motion of feelings, rising and falling, hurrying and lingering. The beauty of music lies in its form. Igor Stravinsky went further in his autobiography of 1936, saying that music is by its nature essentially powerless to express anything at all, though he later qualified the remark.",
      "Others defend expression. Program music, such as Hector Berlioz's Symphonie fantastique (1830), follows a story the composer supplies, while absolute music, such as a string quartet, has no such program. The philosopher Peter Kivy argued that music can be expressive of sadness the way a drooping face or posture looks sad, by resemblance, without anyone being sad. The music theorist Leonard Meyer argued in 1956 that musical emotion and meaning arise from expectations that a piece sets up, delays and fulfils. How listeners hear major and minor keys, or which sounds seem consonant, partly depends on the musical culture they grow up in.",
      "Meaning can also be contested. Dmitri Shostakovich's Fifth Symphony (1937), written after Soviet officials had denounced his music, was received as a triumphant return to favour. Some listeners hear its loud finale as forced rejoicing under pressure; the evidence for this, including the disputed memoir Testimony, is debated. John Cage's 4'33\" (1952), in which performers play nothing and the audience hears the sounds around them, asks whether music requires any intended sound at all."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "expression-or-form", "title": "Decide whether music expresses or only forms", "description": "Compares Hanslick's formalism with expression theories and defends a view.",
        "concept": "musical-formalism", "definition": "The view, defended by Hanslick, that music's content is its moving forms and it cannot represent definite emotions.",
        "facts": ["Hanslick held that music's content is tonally moving forms.", "He argued definite emotions require thoughts about objects that music cannot supply.", "Stravinsky said music is essentially powerless to express anything, though he later qualified this.", "A strong answer explains why so many listeners hear emotion in music anyway."],
        "misconceptions": ["Music simply records what the composer was feeling while writing.", "If music cannot state facts, it has no meaning at all."] },
      { "key": "sad-music", "title": "Explain how music can be sad", "description": "Evaluates explanations of musical expressiveness such as resemblance and expectation.",
        "concept": "musical-expressiveness", "definition": "The way music can sound sad or joyful even though music itself has no feelings.",
        "facts": ["Kivy compared musical sadness to the sad look of a drooping face or posture.", "Meyer traced musical meaning to expectations set up, delayed and fulfilled.", "Hearing major and minor keys emotionally is partly shaped by musical culture.", "A strong answer separates music expressing sadness from music making a listener sad."],
        "misconceptions": ["Minor keys are sad for everyone in every culture.", "Music is sad only if the listener feels sad."] },
      { "key": "contested-meaning", "title": "Judge who decides what a piece means", "description": "Uses cases such as Shostakovich's Fifth to argue whether meaning lies with composer, context or listener.",
        "concept": "contested-meaning", "definition": "Disagreement over a piece's meaning when the composer's intentions, the historical context and listeners' hearings conflict.",
        "facts": ["Shostakovich wrote the Fifth Symphony in 1937 after official denunciation.", "It was received as triumphant, while some hear the finale as forced rejoicing.", "The memoir Testimony, often cited for the ironic reading, is of disputed authenticity.", "A strong answer weighs evidence of intention against the sound and the context."],
        "misconceptions": ["A piece means only what its composer intended.", "Because a piece can be heard many ways, no hearing is better supported than another."] },
      { "key": "music-character", "title": "Assess whether music shapes character", "description": "Evaluates Plato's claim that music forms character and whether it should be regulated.",
        "concept": "music-and-character", "definition": "Plato's view that musical modes shape the soul, so a well-ordered city should control which music is allowed.",
        "facts": ["In the Republic Plato would keep modes that encourage courage and self-control and ban others.", "The view treats music as a matter of public concern.", "Modern debates about the effects of songs on listeners echo the question.", "A strong answer separates the claim that music influences us from the claim that it should be censored."],
        "misconceptions": ["Music is pure entertainment with no effect on character.", "If music influences character, the state should control it."] }
